+
+### Setting-by-Setting Technical Paragraphs
+
+- **provider_kind:** Specifies the authentication protocol implemented by the card. Permitted values are `LOCAL_ADMIN` (argon2id hashed platform administrative credentials), `OIDC` (OpenID Connect discovery and authorization code flow), `OAUTH2` (generic OAuth 2.0 provider with userinfo endpoint), and `LDAP` (Active Directory and OpenLDAP directory integration). The provider kind determines schema validation rules, secret custody requirements, and token minting behavior.
+- **slug:** The unique URL-safe identifier for the authentication card. Must conform to `^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,46}[a-z0-9]$` between 3 and 48 characters. The slug forms the path parameter in public login and callback routes, including `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/start`, `/api/v1/auth/oidc/{slug}/callback`, and `/api/v1/auth/ldap/{slug}/login`. Immutable after creation to maintain bookmark and redirect stability.
+- **display_name:** The user-facing label rendered on the login card button on `/login` (1 to 100 characters). For example, `"Corporate Active Directory"` or `"Google Workspace SSO"`. Provides clear identification of the identity source for end users.
+- **button_subtext:** Optional secondary guidance rendered in smaller typography directly below the card title (up to 200 characters). Commonly used to specify login formats, such as `"Use your firstname.lastname@example.com account"` or `"Hardware security key required"`.
+- **icon_preset:** Visual icon token rendered on the authentication card button. Supported values include `key` (generic credential), `shield` (security key / passkey), `google` (Google colored G), `github` (GitHub octocat), `microsoft` (Windows logo), `ldap` (directory tree), and `terminal` (administrative root). Enhances visual scannability on multi-card login surfaces.
+- **is_enabled:** Master toggle controlling whether the authentication card is active on the `/login` screen. When set to `false`, the card is hidden from the login container, and any authentication attempts targeting the card's routes are immediately rejected with HTTP 403 `FORBIDDEN`. Mutations toggling `is_enabled` enforce anti-lockout validation.
+- **sort_rank:** Fractional rank order string governing the visual sequence of cards on the `/login` container. Cards with lower numeric ranks appear first. Administrators use this setting to position primary corporate SSO providers at the top while keeping break-glass local admin cards accessible at the bottom.
+- **auto_provision_users:** When enabled (`true`), an authenticated user whose external identity claim is verified but does not yet have an account in 👍kanban is automatically provisioned with a new local user record and assigned the workspace default role. When disabled (`false`), external users must be explicitly invited before logging in.
+- **allowed_email_domains:** A security restriction array of permitted corporate email domains (e.g., `["corp.example.com", "subsidiary.com"]`). During OIDC token exchange or LDAP authentication, if the user's verified email address domain does not match any entry in this list, authentication is aborted with HTTP 403 `DOMAIN_NOT_ALLOWED`.
+- **public_config:** A JSON dictionary containing non-sensitive provider configuration attributes. Stored in PostgreSQL `jsonb` columns and safe to expose to administrative API callers. Includes discovery URLs, client IDs, LDAP hosts, user search filters, and group mapping definitions.
+- **secret:** Client secrets, LDAP bind passwords, and private key strings. Inbound payloads accept this write-only parameter, which is immediately encrypted at rest using AES-256-GCM envelope encryption with the platform `MASTER_KEK`. The secret is never returned in any REST API response or serialization model.
+- **google_oauth_json:** A convenience payload accepting the raw client secret JSON file downloaded directly from the Google Cloud Console (up to 32,768 characters). The backend parses the JSON, validates client ID, client secret, redirect URI alignment, and populates `public_config` without manual copy-pasting.
+- **ldap_host:** The fully qualified domain name or IP address of the target directory server. Plaintext LDAP on unencrypted port 389 is rejected by backend security policies; all connections must negotiate TLS.
+- **ldap_port:** Network port for directory services. Defaults to port `636` for LDAPS (implicit SSL/TLS) and port `389` when `ldap_security_mode` is set to `starttls`.
+- **ldap_security_mode:** Transport encryption mode for directory traffic. Permitted values are `ldaps` (direct TLS handshake upon TCP socket connection on port 636) and `starttls` (explicit StartTLS extended operation negotiation on port 389 prior to issuing bind requests).
+- **ca_cert_pem:** Custom PEM-formatted X.509 certificate authority bundle. When connecting to enterprise directory controllers utilizing private internal PKI, administrators paste the issuing root and intermediate CA certificates here. The LDAP client validates the server certificate strictly against this bundle, rejecting untrusted certificates.
+- **bind_dn:** The Distinguished Name of the service account used by 👍kanban to execute user lookup and group resolution queries (e.g., `CN=KanbanService,OU=ServiceAccounts,DC=corp,DC=com`). Must possess read-only search permissions over the target user and group subtrees.
+- **base_dn:** The root Distinguished Name of the directory subtree searched for user accounts (e.g., `OU=Employees,DC=corp,DC=com`). Scopes directory queries to prevent scanning unrelated machine or organizational containers.
+- **user_search_filter:** LDAP filter expression utilized to resolve the input login username to a specific Active Directory user object. The token `{username}` is safely escaped and substituted into the filter (e.g., `(&(objectCategory=person)(sAMAccountName={username}))` or `(&(objectCategory=person)(userPrincipalName={username}))`).
+- **group_search_base:** The root Distinguished Name of the directory subtree containing security groups. Used during group synchronization to locate groups mapped to 👍kanban roles.
+- **group_search_filter:** LDAP filter expression utilized to determine all security groups containing the authenticated user. In Active Directory environments, setting this to `(&(objectCategory=group)(member:1.2.840.113556.1.4.1941:={user_dn}))` leverages the LDAP Matching Rule in Chain (`LDAP_MATCHING_RULE_IN_CHAIN`) to traverse nested group memberships recursively.
+- **sync_nested_groups:** Boolean toggle enabling automatic recursive resolution of nested Active Directory groups. When enabled, users inherit 👍kanban roles associated with parent groups even if their account is only directly assigned to a child group in the directory.
+- **issuer_url:** The standard OpenID Connect discovery base URL (e.g., `https://accounts.google.com` or `https://keycloak.corp.example.com/realms/enterprise`). The backend appends `/.well-known/openid-configuration` to retrieve endpoint URLs, signing algorithms, and JSON Web Key Sets (JWKS).
+- **client_id:** The public OAuth client identifier issued by the identity provider during application registration.
+- **client_secret:** The confidential OAuth client secret credential issued by the identity provider. Held strictly in write-only custody and encrypted via AES-256-GCM.
+- **scopes:** Space-separated OAuth scope list requested during authorization. Defaults to `"openid email profile"`. Scopes must guarantee delivery of the `sub` and `email` claims.
+- **redirect_uri:** The immutable callback URI derived from the deployment hostname and card slug: `https://<domain>/api/v1/auth/oidc/<slug>/callback`. Must be registered verbatim in the identity provider console.
+- **require_email_verified:** Mandatory boolean safety check. When true, the ID token claim `email_verified` must be strictly `true`. If the claim is `false` or missing, authentication is aborted with HTTP 403 `EMAIL_NOT_VERIFIED` to prevent email spoofing attacks.
+- **rp_name:** WebAuthn Relying Party display name. Hardcoded to the canonical platform brand `👍kanban` across all WebAuthn registration and authentication credential requests. Displayed by operating system biometric dialogs and hardware tokens.
+- **rp_id:** WebAuthn Relying Party domain identifier. Formatted as a strict ASCII Fully Qualified Domain Name matching the browser window origin without protocol, port, or trailing slashes (e.g., `kanban.example.com`). Prevents cross-domain credential phishing.
+- **totp_secret:** High-entropy base32 encoded secret key generated during TOTP authenticator enrollment (`POST /api/v1/auth/mfa/totp/enroll`). Encrypted at rest and used to validate 6-digit RFC 6238 time-step codes.
+- **recovery_codes:** Array of single-use backup recovery codes minted during MFA enrollment. Stored in PostgreSQL exclusively as cryptographic salted hashes. Used for break-glass account recovery when hardware tokens or authenticator apps are unavailable.
+- **websudo_duration_seconds:** The lifetime of an elevated Authenticator Assurance Level 2 (AAL2) session window (default 900 seconds / 15 minutes). All administrative endpoints (`/api/v1/admin/*`) require active AAL2 verification. When expired, the frontend prompts for step-up elevation via WebSudo without discarding dirty form state.
+- **lockout_prevention_layer:** Multi-layered defense preventing administrative lockouts. Evaluated at both the PostgreSQL trigger layer (`db_trigger`) and application service layer (`handshake_probe_15m`). Prohibits disabling or deleting the last active administrator card unless an alternative external card has passed a live handshake probe within the last 15 minutes (`HANDSHAKE_PROBE_MAX_AGE_SECONDS = 900`).
+- **rbac_scope_tier:** The administrative boundary defining where a custom RBAC role applies. Allowed values are `system admin` (global platform governance), `workspace admin` (workspace-wide settings, members, and custom fields), and `project admin` (project-specific workflows, WIP limits, and issue operations). Roles in higher tiers cannot be edited by administrators in lower tiers.
+- **rbac_permissions:** The set of granular permissions assigned to a custom RBAC role, selected from the 30 canonical keys across Workspace (8), Issue (10), and Field (12) tiers. Evaluated alongside the 8-bit capability bitmask with absolute DENY precedence (`effective = allow & (~deny)`).
 
